1
00:00:06,878 --> 00:00:08,921
If you could wave a magic wand,

2
00:00:08,963 --> 00:00:13,384
what’s the one idea you’d most love
to spread to other people’s minds?

3
00:00:13,718 --> 00:00:18,097
Through the power of language—
carefully used— we can do just that.

4
00:00:18,347 --> 00:00:22,143
But what’s the most direct way of
gifting an idea to an audience?

5
00:00:22,435 --> 00:00:23,728
Simply show it.

6
00:00:23,853 --> 00:00:28,232
Incorporating visuals can make
a talk more powerful and convincing.

7
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:31,986
Assuming you have your throughline
and talk content,

8
00:00:31,986 --> 00:00:34,155
it's time to get this show on the road.

9
00:00:34,572 --> 00:00:38,659
In the 21st century, we can add
to the spoken word with many technologies—

10
00:00:38,951 --> 00:00:44,415
pictures, illustrations, graphs, charts,
animation, video, audio, and data.

11
00:00:44,999 --> 00:00:49,253
All can help explain your idea
and make your talk visually interesting.

12
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:53,966
But first, ask yourself
whether you actually need visuals.

13
00:00:54,258 --> 00:00:58,429
At least a third of TED’s most-viewed
talks don’t use slides at all.

14
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:01,516
Slides move attention
away from the speaker.

15
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:05,561
If your talk relies on personal
connection, humor, or stories,

16
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:08,856
you may be better off
speaking directly to the audience.

17
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:11,484
For every speaker, the rule is simple:

18
00:01:11,651 --> 00:01:15,488
having no slides is better
than having bad slides.

19
00:01:15,696 --> 00:01:18,533
But many talks benefit
from strong visuals.

20
00:01:18,658 --> 00:01:22,620
Sometimes, the visuals
make the talk a success.

21
00:01:23,287 --> 00:01:27,125
Key components of strong visuals
fall into three categories:

22
00:01:27,375 --> 00:01:32,380
revelation, explanatory power,
and aesthetic appeal.

23
00:01:33,256 --> 00:01:38,261
Revelation: The most obvious reason
to use visuals is to show something

24
00:01:38,261 --> 00:01:39,804
hard to describe.

25
00:01:39,929 --> 00:01:44,892
Edith Widder was part of the team that
first captured the giant squid on video.

26
00:01:45,226 --> 00:01:48,771
Her talk was built around
that moment of revelation.

27
00:01:48,938 --> 00:01:51,232
When the creature appeared on screen,

28
00:01:51,232 --> 00:01:54,152
the audience nearly
jumped out of its skin.

29
00:01:54,861 --> 00:01:57,155
Revelation doesn't have to be dramatic.

30
00:01:57,321 --> 00:02:00,116
Explain any background,
prime the audience,

31
00:02:00,283 --> 00:02:02,743
and then let the visuals do their work.

32
00:02:03,035 --> 00:02:06,205
Run them full screen
without adding anything.

33
00:02:07,165 --> 00:02:13,546
Explanatory power: Words and images
often explain best together.

34
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:16,424
Much of our world is imagined visually.

35
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:21,179
To explain something new, showing
and telling often works best.

36
00:02:21,470 --> 00:02:24,515
But the visual must fit
the spoken message.

37
00:02:25,016 --> 00:02:27,727
Complicated slides can
distract an audience.

38
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:30,938
Limit each slide to one core idea.

39
00:02:31,355 --> 00:02:35,359
Multiple streams of information
require mental energy to process.

40
00:02:35,776 --> 00:02:38,905
A speaker must direct attention
so slides support,

41
00:02:42,283 --> 00:02:45,286
For graphs and charts,
highlight the relevant parts.

42
00:02:45,453 --> 00:02:50,249
If comparing February and October
rainfall, use colors to show the months.

43
00:02:50,583 --> 00:02:53,127
Don't cram everything onto one slide.

44
00:02:53,544 --> 00:02:57,340
Headlines with long bullet points
often lose attention—

45
00:02:57,548 --> 00:03:01,928
turn bullets into separate slides,
reduce phrases,

46
00:03:02,094 --> 00:03:04,138
or replace them with images.

47
00:03:04,513 --> 00:03:07,934
Even simple slides may steal your thunder.

48
00:03:08,434 --> 00:03:13,564
Instead of “A black hole is an object
so massive that no light can escape,”

49
00:03:13,856 --> 00:03:17,026
try: “How black is a black hole?”

50
00:03:17,318 --> 00:03:20,112
Then deliver the information
through speech;

51
00:03:20,488 --> 00:03:25,159
The slide teases curiosity
and makes words more compelling.

52
00:03:26,118 --> 00:03:30,206
Visuals shouldn't communicate
words your mouth can easily say.

53
00:03:30,915 --> 00:03:33,459
Use them to share what speech can’t:

54
00:03:33,626 --> 00:03:37,171
photographs, video, animation, or data.

55
00:03:37,713 --> 00:03:42,009
Screens can explain instantly
what might take hours to describe.

56
00:03:42,677 --> 00:03:47,807
Aesthetic appeal: Nice-looking slides
add power.

57
00:03:48,516 --> 00:03:51,978
Visually appealing slides
can amplify a talk's impact.

58
00:03:52,311 --> 00:03:55,356
Hans Rosling is a TED speaker
who excelled at this.

59
00:03:55,398 --> 00:03:57,692
“Here we go. Can you see there?
It’s China.

60
00:03:57,692 --> 00:04:00,152
They’re moving — better health.
They are improving.

61
00:04:00,152 --> 00:04:02,613
There, all the green
Latin American countries.

62
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:04,615
They are moving towards smaller families.”

63
00:04:04,615 --> 00:04:08,661
Not everyone can be a Hans Rosling,
but every speaker can ask:

64
00:04:08,786 --> 00:04:12,206
are visuals essential
for explaining my idea?

65
00:04:12,707 --> 00:04:15,835
And how can I combine
them effectively with words?

66
00:04:16,711 --> 00:04:19,422
Once you plan your visuals,
revisit your words

67
00:04:19,422 --> 00:04:23,301
and figure out how to turn them
into a rewarding presentation.

68
00:04:23,968 --> 00:04:28,973
All these lessons should put you
on your way to delivering a powerful talk—

69
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:35,229
one that inspires, delights,
and may even change the world.

